     not been following the stages. It leads with what they have to do and only then reports
     what is already in place — the reverse of `starter-readiness`, which is the same core
     field set written for the people who did the provisioning.
     Deliberately not a lighter document than the one it sits beside: see the gate strictness
     note in v1's definition.yaml for why the shortest artefact at a gate must not be the
     easiest.

     v2 (#90): named from the candidate and today's date (definition.yaml's `filename:`) —
     unlike the other candidate-named documents this one carries `{today}` rather than the
     start date, since it can be re-sent close to the start date and the date it was produced
     is the more useful fact for a manager filing it. `selection.candidate-name` is `?` in
     `requires`, purely so the pattern can reach it — this document carries no other selection
     content, matching v1. `contract.start-date` is formatted with `formatDate()`. */
